# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>. The last field is prose, which is why the separator is a tab.
#
# **This file is the other half of `tools/corpus/VERSIONS`, and the two pin
# opposite directions.** That file pins the *corpora* - which commit of
# test262, which release of perl's `re_tests` - and it is honest about pinning
# them to whatever Debian 13 gives: "from the release that matches the
# pcre2test on this machine", "the ref matches the glibc on this machine".
# This file pins the *reference that answers*, so that the two can be moved
# deliberately and together. A corpus imported from one version and answered
# by another measures the gap between them and reports it as a defect here.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee (CONTAINERS.md section
# 2.6). A stock image is pinned by digest and the digest is the whole
# guarantee: a tag can be moved under you and a digest cannot, so a green run
# names a set of bytes rather than a set of names. An image **built here**
# cannot be - two builds of one Dockerfile are not two copies of one image -
# so what is pinned instead is everything the build reads, and the run-time
# version check is the real guarantee.
#
# The version field is what the reference must say it is when asked, and it
# names **what decides an answer** rather than what the package is called.
# For a reference carrying bundled Unicode tables that is the data version:
# `tools/corpus/VERSIONS` records that vim, CPython and node were in daily use
# here with no pinned record at all, answering 177,580 rows a run between
# them, and that a reference vendoring its own tables carries a third version
# independent of both this library's and the standard's.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate, and is its own commit. A new reference version
# is new answers, and a new answer that disagrees is a finding to triage
# rather than a regression to fix - so containerising a reference and raising
# it are two changes, and doing both at once means the next disagreement
# cannot be attributed to either.

# perl, for the Perl-family front end and for four tables it is the only
# reference to. `GRX_SYNTAX_PERL` means "what perl does" rather than what a
# document says, so this is a definition rather than a second opinion.
#
# **5.40.1 because that is where the corpus came from.** `tools/corpus/VERSIONS`
# pins `perl v5.40.1` and `tests/data/vectors/perl/` was imported from that
# release's `re_tests`; this pin and that one move together or not at all.
# Debian 13 ships 5.40.1 too, so this entry changes no answer on this machine -
# which is the point of landing it before the raise below.
perl	docker.io/library/perl:5.40.1-slim@sha256:741d8a6d6e1aef546c29fb55173d6c7db1205f603e4d7b968186c7fc96b6126c	perl v5.40.1	Perl-family match and syntax, case-fold orbits, Numeric_Value, script runs, splitting and iteration

# A second perl, not a replacement, and the payoff CONTAINERS.md section 2.3
# names. 5.40.1 carries UCD 15.0.0 and these tables are 17.0.0, so three
# segmentation rules exist here that this perl has never seen - GB9c (15.1),
# LB15c (16) and LB21a's HH (17) - and **46 rows of
# tests/data/vectors/perl/boundaries.rxt are excluded** for that reason, each
# named in tools/corpus/make_boundary_vectors.py. 5.44.0 carries UCD 17.0.0
# exactly, which is what retires them.
#
# Measured rather than assumed, through this image:
#
#   perl -e 'my @g = ("\x{915}\x{94D}\x{937}" =~ /(\X)/g); print scalar @g'
#   # 5.40.1 -> 2     5.44.0 -> 1     (GB9c, Unicode 15.1)
#
# Debian 13 ships 5.40.1 and only one perl can be installed, so before this
# file existed there was no way to ask the second one anything.
#
#   GHOTI_ORACLE_ALIAS=perl=perl-next make check-oracle-perl-syntax
#
# It is **not** the gating pin, and the reason is the corpus rather than the
# release: raising the pin here without re-importing `re_tests` and
# regenerating the boundary vectors would answer a 5.40.1 corpus with a 5.44.0
# perl. What it is for is the reading between the two.
